window.__posterData({"id": "alice-mcpherson-stahl", "name": "Alice McPherson-Ståhl", "folder": "Week44_AI_Poster_Alice_Mcpherson-Ståhl", "v": "2", "scripts": ["https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5@2.3.4/lib/p5.js", "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5.sound@0.4.1/dist/p5.sound.min.js"], "links": [], "css": "html, body {\n  margin: 0;\n  padding: 0;\n}\ncanvas {\n  display: block;\n}\n", "code": "// QUESTIONS\n// -----Step One:\n// Has it changed? \n  // Yes the code has changed, the look of the poster hasn't.\n\n// How much shorter, or longer, is the code?\n  // The AI code is 45 rows longer. \n\n// Did it do things that you understand, or cover new\n// concepts we have not in class?\n// IMAGES\n   //  A new concept to me that the AI did was for the images. My own code involved declaring variables for each image in the form of an array at the start. In the setup function they are then later declared with await promise all to load all the images at once, and then loadImage and the filepath for each image. These are written in the same order as the variables so that the program knows that titleSquare = components/titlesquare.png and so on. \n  //  This is the code the AI wrote. I don't understand this at all really:\n      // imageNames.map(name =>\n      // loadImage(`components/${name}.png`)\n      // imageNames.forEach((name, index) => {\n      // images[name] = loadedImages[index];\n// FUNCTIONS\n   // The AI Code includes multiple functions where the function name is created by the AI and not predefined syntax. I have only used predefined syntax such as function draw, setup, keypressed and more. All of my interaction code is also written in the same function and then refers to two separate input functions. One of which includes two separate keyboard keys. The AI still defines these two separate input functions, I.E keypressed and mousepressed. But for what actually happens when these inputs are made, for example pressing the key S, the actions are in separate functions. For example function drawGlitchBoard and function drawTitleAnimation. This is something I can understand, however to me it seems more complicated. With my version everything is in the same place but can still be called in separately. But the AI version divides everything up, which I can't explain any further other than it just feels more complicated in my head. I do wonder however, which version is more effective for the browser to actually load. I can imagine, with my version, it may mean the browser has to load all of the code even for the parts that are not being used. By this I mean even for input actions that are not actively being displayed. \n// THE STARS\n  // It uses a different method for making the stars spin. In my code the stars spinning is in the form of an if statement. If = draw the stars on the canvas in a set position, no movement. Else = places the star on the page with a rotation of 0.05 per frame around its x and y coordinate original location. The AI code uses an if statement but no else. If = draws the stars on the canvas in a set position just like mine, it then finishes off with return which I don't know what it means. After the if statement there are five, what I think might be functions, that pick out the image and set a position. After all of these it simply redefines the variable starRotation from its previous 0 to + 0.25. I have no clue how the browser understands that the variable star rotation should be applied to those stars as it hasn't been explicitly 'told' in any other part of the code. My thought is that it is enough that there is a separate function called drawStars and that then means the browser by default understands that the change in value for the variable starRotation should be applied to the code in the function. I wonder if Return could have anything to do with this too. It could be to 'tell' the browser to apply the new value of the variable to everything after return in the function drawStars.  \n// KERNING\n  // The kerning section is much shorter. The code for kerning in my code is in a function just like the AI code, it starts off by adding blank characters between each letter in a string. It then adds a spacer, splits the string into a list of characters, creates a series of characters through repeat and then joins the characters with the spacer to finally send back as a string. The AI creates a function called addLetterSpacing and then writes: \n // input,\n //  amount,\n //  spacer = \"\\u200A\"\n// return input\n//     .split(\"\")\n//     .join(spacer.repeat(amount));\n// I honestly have no clue how this works, since, and I might just have missed it, but I can't see that any of the code is referred to anywhere else in the code. Meaning I don't know how the browser knows to actually apply this stuff to the text. I think input, amount and spacing might be variables. \n\n// How do you feel about it?\n  // I don't feel any certain way about any of this and I don't know what else I can express for this topic.\n\n// --- Step 2: \n// The change that was made was changing the size from 382 x 540 to 600 x 800 and moving and resizing the elements on the canvas accordingly.\n\n// --- Step 3: \n// Do you like it?\n// Not really, no.\n\n// Does it fit with your overall aesthetic? Why or why not?\n// The colours fit, however because of the tight spacing between the stars and the general convention it's a bit too sparkly for the overall vibe. \n\n// Design Critique\n// PLAYABILITY\n// The function does invite interactivity because as soon as you touch the mouse the stars are shown. This I think might give the viewer or user an impression that there could be more which will lead the user to try other inputs to see if the poster can do anything else. In this sense it does follow the definition given to us in class, that it invites or encourages further interaction with the poster.\n// ELEGANCE\n// I would argue that this is both elegant and not. Looking at the poster as a whole it had a lot of elements with a lot of movement when all elements are active. However looking at the input on its own it can be quite elegant, it has simple colours, and works in a very simple, slightly predictable way. On the other hand, looking at it on its own, the actual code for it also seems quite busy and slightly complex. \n// SURPRISE\n// The function has an element of surprise. A most probable first interaction would be moving the mouse. Because of this the first direct feedback to user interaction is stars that move around with the mouse. Users who are not familiar with the assignment would probably not expect anything to happen and because of this might be surprised when something actually does. On top of this I think it can be surprising because of  the fact that the function technically doesn't align with the 'aesthetic'. And thus is unexpected due to a humans naturally set up expectations based on what they know about grunge, punk or in general alternative style. \n// IDENTITY\n// The poster's identity is very much along the lines of an alternative subculture. This signals to the viewer that even though the poster has information about the event, not everyone will enjoy the event due to the commonly associated music and culture. The poster is not meant to be unwelcoming but instead meant for the user to themselves decide if they are welcome or not. The function that the AI has added, I feel, could both add to and remove some of this quality. As I previously stated, the function, even though they are stars, look more like sparkles to me. Since I am working with the users own associations with alternative style, sparkles are not something commonly associated with an otherwise scary slightly unfamiliar culture. This might mean the poster can be viewed as more leaning towards inviting instead of you're invited if you feel it's your thing. On the other hand the sparkles could actually be a good thing. Alternative culture, for those who are not a part of it can be slightly scary. The core of which the culture is built around is sticking out and being an individual, a scary concept for some. And the way people within the culture present themselves, as well as other mediums such as angry heavy music, can also be scary or offputting. However despite the common expectations from alternative culture, its people are most often very loving, including and kind. Just look at angry fat bikers with tattoos and long beards. If you ever ask one of them for help they will be very happy to assist. Because of this the playful look of the sparkles still manages to reflect that unknown fact. We are not as scary as we may look or seem. Or in my case, I'm not as scary as my music taste may make you think I am.  \n \n\n// =====================================================\n// VARIABLES\n// =====================================================\n\nconst images = {};\n\nlet time = 0;\nlet motion = false;\n\nlet starRotation = 0;\nlet spin = false;\n\nlet glitch = false;\n\nconst size = 500;\nlet angle = 0;\n\nlet oswaldFont;\n\nconst DESIGN_WIDTH = 382;\nconst DESIGN_HEIGHT = 540;\n\n// Canvas matches the artwork's proportions so it fills the screen with no gap\nconst CANVAS_HEIGHT = 800;\nconst CANVAS_WIDTH = Math.round(CANVAS_HEIGHT * DESIGN_WIDTH / DESIGN_HEIGHT);\n\n// ---- Sparkle trail settings ----\nlet sparkles = [];            // all the little stars currently alive\nlet designScale = 1;          // saved each frame so the mouse lines up with the artwork\nconst MAX_SPARKLES = 80;      // most stars allowed at once\nconst SPARKLE_SIZE = 7;       // size of each little star\nconst SPARKLE_FADE = 0.025;   // higher = disappear faster\n// --------------------------------\n\nconst imageNames = [\n  \"titlesquare\",\n  \"title\",\n  \"birthdaycelebration\",\n  \"bigstar\",\n  \"mediumstar\",\n  \"smallstar\",\n  \"stilltext\",\n  \"ministar\",\n  \"gameboard\",\n  \"bigmommatext\",\n  \"includetext\"\n];\n\n// =====================================================\n// SETUP\n// =====================================================\n\nasync function setup() {\n\n  createCanvas(CANVAS_WIDTH, CANVAS_HEIGHT);\n\n  const loadedImages = await Promise.all(\n    imageNames.map(name =>\n      loadImage(`components/${name}.png`)\n    )\n  );\n\n  imageNames.forEach((name, index) => {\n    images[name] = loadedImages[index];\n  });\n\n  oswaldFont = await loadFont(\"Oswald-Bold.ttf\");\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// DRAW\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction draw() {\n\n  background(32, 31, 33);\n\n  const scaleFactor = min(\n    width / DESIGN_WIDTH,\n    height / DESIGN_HEIGHT\n  );\n\n  designScale = scaleFactor;\n\n  push();\n\n  // Keep artwork anchored top-left\n  scale(scaleFactor);\n\n  drawGlitchBoard();\n\n  image(images.stilltext, 0, 0);\n  image(images.titlesquare, 0, 0);\n\n  drawTitleAnimation();\n\n  drawStars();\n\n  drawInstructions();\n\n  image(images.includetext, 0, 0);\n\n  // Sparkles go last so they sit on top of everything\n  drawSparkles();\n\n  pop();\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// GLITCH EFFECT\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction drawGlitchBoard() {\n\n  if (!glitch) {\n    image(images.gameboard, 0, 0);\n    return;\n  }\n\n  let offset = random(-10, 10);\n\n  tint(255, 0, 0);\n  image(images.gameboard, 0, offset);\n\n  tint(0, 255, 0);\n  image(images.gameboard, 0, -offset);\n\n  tint(0, 0, 255);\n  image(images.gameboard, offset, 0);\n\n  noTint();\n  image(images.gameboard, offset, 0);\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// TITLE + BIG MOMMA MOTION\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction drawTitleAnimation() {\n\n  if (!motion) {\n\n    image(images.title, 0, 0);\n    image(images.birthdaycelebration, 0, 0);\n\n    fill(250, 171, 83);\n    rectMode(CENTER);\n\n    rect(\n      223,\n      515,\n      290,\n      30\n    );\n\n    image(\n      images.bigmommatext,\n      100,\n      489,\n      280,\n      50\n    );\n\n    return;\n  }\n\n  let y = sin(time) * 5;\n\n  image(images.title, 0, y);\n  image(images.birthdaycelebration, 0, y);\n\n  time += 0.5;\n\n  let x = sin(time) * 1.5;\n\n  image(images.birthdaycelebration, x, 0);\n\n  time += 0.5;\n\n  let grow = sin(angle) * ((size / 20) * y);\n\n  push();\n\n  fill(250, 171, 83);\n  rectMode(CENTER);\n\n  rect(\n    228,\n    513,\n    380,\n    grow\n  );\n\n  pop();\n\n  image(\n    images.bigmommatext,\n    100,\n    489,\n    280,\n    50\n  );\n\n  angle += 0.09;\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// STARS\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction drawStars() {\n\n  if (!spin) {\n\n    image(images.bigstar, -120, 360);\n    image(images.mediumstar, 300, 150);\n    image(images.smallstar, 50, 110);\n    image(images.ministar, 95, 3);\n    image(images.ministar, 265, 3);\n\n    return;\n  }\n\n  drawRotatingStar(\n    images.bigstar,\n    30,\n    520\n  );\n\n  drawRotatingStar(\n    images.smallstar,\n    102,\n    160\n  );\n\n  drawRotatingStar(\n    images.mediumstar,\n    380,\n    235\n  );\n\n  drawRotatingStar(\n    images.ministar,\n    105,\n    13\n  );\n\n  drawRotatingStar(\n    images.ministar,\n    278,\n    13\n  );\n\n  starRotation += 0.25;\n}\n\nfunction drawRotatingStar(img, x, y) {\n\n  push();\n\n  translate(x, y);\n\n  rotate(starRotation);\n\n  imageMode(CENTER);\n\n  image(img, 0, 0);\n\n  pop();\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// SPARKLE TRAIL (mouse)\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction drawSparkles() {\n\n  // Is the mouse on the poster, and is it actually moving?\n  const onCanvas =\n    mouseX >= 0 && mouseX <= width &&\n    mouseY >= 0 && mouseY <= height;\n\n  const moved = dist(mouseX, mouseY, pmouseX, pmouseY);\n\n  // Make 2 new sparkles while the mouse moves\n  if (onCanvas && moved > 2) {\n\n    for (let i = 0; i < 2; i++) {\n\n      sparkles.push({\n        // Mouse position converted into the artwork's coordinates\n        x: mouseX / designScale + random(-4, 4),\n        y: mouseY / designScale + random(-4, 4),\n        speedX: random(-0.4, 0.4),\n        speedY: random(0.2, 0.8),\n        rotation: random(TWO_PI),\n        spinSpeed: random(-0.15, 0.15),\n        life: 1\n      });\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Keep the list from getting too long\n  while (sparkles.length > MAX_SPARKLES) {\n    sparkles.shift();\n  }\n\n  // Update and draw each sparkle (loop backwards so we can safely remove)\n  for (let i = sparkles.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {\n\n    const s = sparkles[i];\n\n    // Drift down and spin\n    s.x += s.speedX;\n    s.y += s.speedY;\n    s.rotation += s.spinSpeed;\n    s.life -= SPARKLE_FADE;\n\n    // Remove when faded out\n    if (s.life <= 0) {\n      sparkles.splice(i, 1);\n      continue;\n    }\n\n    push();\n\n    translate(s.x, s.y);\n    rotate(s.rotation);\n\n    // Same look as the big stars: purple fill, orange outline\n    fill(143, 100, 160);\n    stroke(250, 171, 83);\n    strokeWeight(1.5);\n    strokeJoin(ROUND);\n\n    // Shrinks as it fades\n    const r = SPARKLE_SIZE * s.life;\n    drawStarShape(0, 0, r * 0.45, r, 5);\n\n    pop();\n  }\n}\n\n// Draws a star with a given number of points\nfunction drawStarShape(x, y, innerRadius, outerRadius, points) {\n\n  const step = TWO_PI / points;\n\n  beginShape();\n\n  for (let a = -HALF_PI; a < TWO_PI - HALF_PI; a += step) {\n\n    vertex(\n      x + cos(a) * outerRadius,\n      y + sin(a) * outerRadius\n    );\n\n    vertex(\n      x + cos(a + step / 2) * innerRadius,\n      y + sin(a + step / 2) * innerRadius\n    );\n  }\n\n  endShape(CLOSE);\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// TEXT\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction drawInstructions() {\n\n  textFont(oswaldFont);\n  textSize(10);\n  textAlign(LEFT, CENTER);\n  noStroke();\n\n  // Same orange as the rest of the poster\n  fill(250, 171, 83);\n\n  const lines = [\n    \"CLICK\",\n    \"THE LETTERS\",\n    \"S, G OR THE\",\n    \"MOUSEPAD\"\n  ];\n\n  // ---- Tweak these three to position the text ----\n  const leftX = 10;        // where the left edge of the text starts\n  const centerY = 508;     // move up / down (middle of the whole text block)\n  const textWidth = 1.5;   // 1 = normal, higher = wider\n  // ------------------------------------------------\n\n  const lineHeight = 12;   // space between lines\n\n  // Works out where the first line goes so the whole block is centred on centerY\n  const startY = centerY - ((lines.length - 1) * lineHeight) / 2;\n\n  push();\n\n  // Move to the left edge, then stretch everything sideways\n  translate(leftX, 0);\n  scale(textWidth, 1);\n\n  lines.forEach((line, i) => {\n    text(line, 0, startY + i * lineHeight);\n  });\n\n  pop();\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// INPUTS\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction mouseClicked() {\n  motion = !motion;\n}\n\nfunction keyPressed() {\n\n  if (key === \"s\") {\n    spin = !spin;\n  }\n\n  else if (key === \"g\") {\n    glitch = !glitch;\n  }\n}\n\n// =====================================================\n// LETTER SPACING\n// =====================================================\n\nfunction addLetterSpacing(\n  input,\n  amount,\n  spacer = \"\\u200A\"\n) {\n\n  return input\n    .split(\"\")\n    .join(spacer.repeat(amount));\n}", "assets": {"Oswald-Bold.ttf": {"mime": "font/ttf", "b64": ""}, "components/titlesquare.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/mediumstar.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/title.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/bigstarbigcanv.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/bigstar.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/gameboard.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/includetext.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/bigmommatext.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/birthdaycelebration.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/smallstar.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/mediumstarbigcanv.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/ministar.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}, "components/stilltext.png": {"mime": "image/webp", "b64": ""}}});
